            "alt": "Refer to caption",
            "desc": "Results\n\n\nPerformance is evaluated exclusively on the masked (held-out) regions of each\ntrajectory.\nWe report in Figure 1 MSE, MAE, and R2R^{2}, each\ncomputed per trajectory and then averaged over the full 1,0001,000-sample dataset (together with the standard deviation over the trajectories).\nThe same figures are given for the baseline algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBaseline:\n\n\nLinear interpolation\n\n\nMetric\nMean ±\\pm Std\n\n\n\n\nMSE\n0.047±0.0960.047\\pm 0.096\n\n\nMAE\n0.159±0.0830.159\\pm 0.083\n\n\nR2\n\n−0.437±5.639-0.437\\pm 5.639\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTCN model\n\n\n\n\nMetric\nMean ±\\pm Std\n\n\nMSE\n0.004±0.0120.004\\pm 0.012\n\n\nMAE\n0.047±0.0250.047\\pm 0.025\n\n\nR2\n\n0.776±0.7770.776\\pm 0.777\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFigure 1: \nLeft top: Quantitative evaluation on masked regions (trained on 1,0001{,}000 synthetic\ntrajectories, 20% mask fraction) for a linear interpolation baseline.\nLeft bottom: Quantitative evaluation on masked regions (trained on 1,0001{,}000 synthetic\ntrajectories, 20% mask fraction).\nRight: Loss evolution on the train and test datasets.\n\n\n\nThe R2R^{2} figure indicates that the model captures most of the dominant\ntemporal structure of the synthetic trajectories. The associated standard\ndeviation reflects the heterogeneity of masked segments, with\nperformance degrading in cases where the gap overlaps regions of high local\ncurvature or rapid directional change. The model shows clear improvement with respect to the baseline in all three metrics.\n\n\nFor comparison, an earlier experiment conducted on a smaller, less varied\ndataset (200200 trajectories drawn from only two underlying trajectory shapes)\nyielded considerably stronger metrics, as shown in Table 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDataset\nMetric\nMean ±\\pm Std\n\n\n\n\nSmooth\nMSE\n0.0011±0.00050.0011\\pm 0.0005\n\n\n\nMAE\n0.025±0.0060.025\\pm 0.006\n\n\n\nR2\n\n0.984±0.0060.984\\pm 0.006\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDataset\nMetric\nMean ±\\pm Std\n\n\n\n\nWiggly\nMSE\n0.0025±0.00090.0025\\pm 0.0009\n\n\n\nMAE\n0.038±0.0070.038\\pm 0.007\n\n\n\nR2\n\n0.965±0.0110.965\\pm 0.011\n\n\n\n\n\nTable 2: Pilot experiment results on the two-trajectory dataset (trained on 200200 samples).\n\n\nFigure 2: True versus predicted trajectories. The predicted trajectories are constructed using true values for the known part and predicted values for the masked part.\n\n\nThe performance gap between the pilot and the full experiment is consistent\nwith the expectation that the 1,000-trajectory dataset introduces harder,\nmore diverse examples that expose the limits of the current architecture and\ntraining configuration. We also examined the median of the per-trajectory scores as a\nmore outlier-robust summary; while it differs from the mean owing to a skewed\nerror distribution, it preserves the same ordering, with our model\noutperforming the baseline on all three metrics.",
